# ctl.<service> control-property labels. NO domain is granted `set` on any of
# them, and that is the whole rule rather than an omission.
#
# An unlabelled ctl.<name> falls through plat_property_contexts:116's `ctl.`
# prefix to ctl_default_prop, and private/system_app.te:62,
# private/system_server.te:595, private/usbd.te:12 and public/wificond.te:12 can
# all set that type. init synthesises the check name as "ctl." + the VALUE of
# ctl.start/ctl.stop/ctl.restart (property_service.cpp CheckControlPropertyPerms
# tries that legacy door first and succeeds if EITHER door passes), so any
# system_app could start and stop these vendor daemons. The newer
# ctl.start$<name> door is already shut: ctl_start_prop, ctl_stop_prop and
# ctl_restart_prop have no `set` grant anywhere in the linked policy.
#
# The setter survey behind "no set_prop": exactly five shipped artifacts in this
# image contain any "ctl." string at all -- vendor/bin/ccci_mdinit,
# vendor/lib/volte_imsm.so, vendor/lib/libmal_rilproxy.so, vendor/bin/gsm0710muxd
# and system/framework/mediatek-telephony-common.jar -- and between them they
# name only services this tree already labels. Every service below is started by
# init from a `class` line, an `on property:` trigger or the `start` builtin, so
# there is nothing to grant. If that ever changes, add exactly one set_prop()
# and name the binary that carries the service-name literal, the way
# radio/ccci_mdinit.te and radio/ims.te do.
#
# Lazy HALs are unaffected: hwservicemanager uses ctl.interface_start$<fqName>,
# which is not one of the three names the legacy door is tried for.
#
# These two matter most. Both domains hold create_file_perms on
# mtk_nvram_storage_file -- which spans mtk_protect_f_file and
# mtk_protect_s_file, the IMEI-bearing protect1/protect2 partitions -- plus raw
# rw_file_perms on mtk_nvram_block_device, mtk_nvdata_block_device and
# mtk_proinfo_block_device.
type ctl_mtk_nvram_daemon_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_nvram_hidl_prop, property_type;
